<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec_lrc" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  	<title>Circuits and Harmonic Motion</title>
	<p>
				A circuit with an inductor and a capacitor, as in the <xref ref="x_fig_LC"/>, is analogous to a simple harmonic oscillator such as the one in the <xref ref="mass_spring"/>.  The exercise below will help you extend this analogy to circuits that also include a resistor.
	</p>

	<activity>
		<title>Harmonic Motion Analogy</title>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				
			</p>
			<p>
				A circuit with a capacitor, a resistor, and an inductor such as the one in the figure below can be modeled using <xref ref="sec_damped"/>.
				
			</p>
			<figure xml:id="RLC1">
			  	<title>LRC Circuit Figure 1</title>
				<caption>A circuit diagram with a resistor, a capacitor, and an inductor.</caption>
				<image source="LRC1.png" width = "50%" ><description>A circuit diagram with a charged capacitor on the left (positive on top), an open switch, and a resistor and an inductor on the right.</description></image>
			</figure>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					A situation exhibiting damped harmonic motion typically has three features: an inertial mass, a restoring force, and a dissipative force.  An example of damped motion is the block on a spring subject to a drag force shown in the <xref ref="mass_spring_damp"/>.
				</p>
				<p>
					Which parts of the circuit above do you think correspond to each part of the damped harmonic motion?
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Sketch a prediction of the current through the circuit as a function of time after the switch is closed.  Use the loop rule to justify your prediction.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Use a graphing calculator to graph the equation of motion for <xref ref="assumption-damped"/> to check your prediction.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					How do you think the behavior of the circuit would change if you included a <alert>battery</alert> in addition to an inductor, a resistor, and a capacitor?  Can you think of a feature of a block on a spring that would be analogous to adding a battery?
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
	</activity>

	
	

	


